[38;5;231m⏺[39m I went with the token option. [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m still requires login, and it now also accepts [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer [39m
  [38;5;153m$REPORTS_TOKEN[39m, so the dashboard script can get in without a session.

  [1mWhat changed:[0m only [38;5;153mauth.js[39m. [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m lets a request through if it has the session cookie or a matching token. The
  token comparison is constant-time. If [38;5;153mREPORTS_TOKEN[39m isn't set, token access stays off, so a missing or empty value
  can't open the route.

  [1mSmoke test[0m against a local server:
  - No credentials: 401
  - Wrong token: 401
  - Correct token: 200 with the report rows
  - Session cookie: 200, as before

  [1mFor you to do:[0m
  1. Set [38;5;153mREPORTS_TOKEN[39m wherever the service is deployed. Until you do, the script will keep getting 401.
  2. Give the dashboard team the same value. Their script needs to send [38;5;153m-H "Authorization: Bearer <token>"[39m.

  Separately, the existing cookie check only looks for any [38;5;153msession=[39m in the cookie header and never verifies a signature,
  so anyone can fake it by sending [38;5;153mCookie: session=x[39m. That was already there before this change, but it's worth fixing.

  I haven't committed anything. Tell me if you want a commit.

